lindp -- linearized (IKKBZ + LinDP) join search
================================================

This test module is a prototype replacement for GEQO that performs join-order
optimization via search-space linearization rather than a genetic algorithm.
It installs a `join_search_hook` and, for join problems with at least
`lindp.threshold` relations, optimizes them as follows:

1. joingraph.c - builds the join graph over the initial relations: per-relation
   cardinalities, adjacency (via have_relevant_joinclause and
   have_join_order_restriction), and surrogate per-edge selectivities (via
   clauselist_selectivity, used only for seeding), plus a connectivity check.

2. ikkbz.c - IKKBZ linearization, computing a good left-deep linear order.
   Builds a precedence forest (spanning tree) per candidate root (so
   disconnected/cross-product graphs still linearize), computes ASI
   ranks under a C_out surrogate cost, and emits the provably-optimal linear
   extension by greedily selecting the free node (parent already emitted)
   with the smallest ASI rank. Tries multiple roots (gated by lindp.effort)
   and keeps the cheapest order.

3. lindp.c - runs LinDP: an exact O(n^3) interval DP over the contiguous
   sub-ranges of the linear order.  Every DP cell is built with the real
   make_join_rel(), so the resulting plan uses PostgreSQL's true cost model
   and full set of paths (hash/merge/nestloop, parameterized, partitionwise,
   parallel, etc). This explores bushy plans within the linearized neighbourhood
   at O(n^3) make_join_rel() calls. Finalizes each range exactly like
   standard_join_search (partitionwise/gather/grouped/set_cheapest, with
   the top-rel exception).

Unlike GEQO, the result is deterministic.

If the join problem is too large (`lindp.max_relations`), the module is
disabled (`lindp.enabled = off`), or the linearization fails to find a legal
complete order (e.g. due to strong outer-join/LATERAL constraints), the module
falls back to the default join search (standard_join_search, or a previously
installed hook).

GUCs
----

    lindp.enabled        (bool, default on)
    lindp.fallback       (bool, default off)
    lindp.min_threshold  (int,  default 12)  -- min relations to use LinDP
    lindp.max_threshold  (int,  default 100) -- max relations to optimize
    lindp.effort         (int,  default -1)  -- higher tries more IKKBZ roots

Glosary
-------

- C_out - A simplified join cost metric used widely in join‑ordering theory.
  For a join order, C_out is the sum of the cardinalities (row counts) of all
  intermediate join results. It ignores per‑algorithm costs (hash vs. merge
  vs. nested loop, I/O, CPU) and just measures how big do the intermediate
  results get. Computed by order_cost() - it walks the linear order, multiplies
  in each relation's cardinality and the selectivities of its join edges to
  track the running intermediate size, and accumulates it into the total.

  It's a stand‑in cost, not the real PostgreSQL cost model. IKKBZ needs a
  cheap, analytically tractable cost function to find a provably optimal
  left‑deep order. C_out is that proxy. Note the resulting order is only
  a seed: LinDP re‑costs it with the real PostgreSQL cost model, so the
  surrogate need not match reality exactly.

  So C_out is a surrogate for the true cost.

- ASI‑conforming" - ASI stands for the "Adjacent Sequence Interchange property",
  evaluating if swapping two adjacent elements in a sequence improves a cost
  function, allowing algorithms to efficiently sort data or determine optimal
  scheduling without checking every possible combination. A cost function is
  ASI‑conforming if you can assign each node a rank such that the optimal
  sequence is found by ordering nodes by that rank - meaning swapping two
  adjacent subsequences improves cost iff their ranks are out of order.

  This is what makes IKKBZ work: the algorithm builds a precedence (spanning)
  tree and greedily emits the free node with the smallest ASI rank to get the
  optimal linear extension (ikkbz.c:86–159). The C_out cost is ASI‑conforming,
  with the rank (see asi_rank) defined as

     rank = (T - 1) / T

  where T = selectivity(parent, node) * card(node)

  Putting it together: "ASI‑conforming C_out surrogate" = a proxy cost
  function (C_out - the sum of intermediate join cardinalities) that has the
  ASI / adjacent‑sequence‑interchange property, so IKKBZ can compute a
  provably optimal left‑deep join order under it via rank‑based ordering.

  It's deliberately approximate because LinDP later re‑evaluates the candidate
  order with PostgreSQL's actual cost model.
